n (did not beat the simpler variant)\",\n512\t        \"from analytic posterior to parametric-bootstrap pivot calibration (under-covered: 0.77)\"\n513\t      ],\n514\t      \"benchmarks\": [\n515\t        \"MMLU-Pro (replayed outcomes, 24 configs, subject codes)\",\n516\t        \"synthetic latent-difficulty worlds (varying N questions, K configs; equal-config null worlds)\"\n517\t      ],\n518\t      \"central_topic\": \"Quantifying and correcting the winner's-curse bias of reporting the best searched LLM prompt/pipeline configuration's score on the same benchmark questions used to select it, via searcher-agnostic debiased interval reporting evaluated on synthetic worlds and replayed MMLU-Pro outcomes.\",\n519\t      \"comparators\": [\n520\t        \"adaptive data analysis / holdout reuse\",\n521\t        \"Ladder leaderboard mechanism\",\n522\t        \"inference on winners and post-selection inference\",\n523\t        \"best-arm-identification / successive-halving prompt selection\",\n524\t        \"DSPy/MIPRO auto-tuners\",\n525\t        \"efficient multi-prompt evaluation and prompt-format sensitivity\"\n526\t      ],\n527\t      \"core_methods\": [\n528\t        \"empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic item-response (latent difficulty) posterio\n529\t\n530\t[truncated after 14000 chars]\n531\t```\n532\t\n533\t## manuscript source excerpt\n534\t\n535\t```tex\n536\t\\documentclass{article}\n537\t\n538\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n539\t\\usepackage{xfp}\n540\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n541\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n542\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{\n543\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n544\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}\n545\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}\n546\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}\n547\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}\n548\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}\n549\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}\n550\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}\n551\t}\n552\t\n553\t \n554\t\\usepackage{paper,times}\n555\t\n556\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n557\t\\usepackage{url}\n558\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n559\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n560\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n561\t\n562\t\\newcommand{\\IS}{\\mathrm{IS}}\n563\t\\newcommand{\\theHat}{\\hat\\theta}\n564\t\n565\t\\title{Reporting Scores After Prompt Search: A Selection-Aware Posterior Interval from a Hierarchical Latent-Difficulty Model}\n566\t\\iclrfinalcopy\n567\t\\author{The Agentic Scientist}\n568\t\n569\t\\begin{document}\n570\t\\maketitle\n571\t\n572\t\\begin{abstract}\n573\tAutomatic prompt and pipeline search reuses one benchmark sample both to choose a configuration and to score it, so the reported score can overstate accuracy on fresh questions.\n574\tWe measure this optimism in controlled worlds with known truth---synthetic latent-difficulty worlds and cached outcomes of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct on MMLU-Pro under 24 prompt configurations, searched exhaustively or by successive halving---and test a searcher-agnostic remedy.\n575\tThe naive best-of-search score overstates fresh-question accuracy by 0.037 on average (about 0.05 at 60 benchmark questions), and its nominal 90\\% Wilson interval contains the fresh accuracy in only 71\\% of replications.\n576\tThe remedy fits an empirical-Bayes hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model to the full search log and reports posterior quantiles.\n577\tIn a sealed evaluation over eight seeds it lowers the mean 90\\%-level interval score to 0.145, against 0.220 (naive), 0.197 (Bonferroni-widened Wilson) and 0.246 (question resampling), with coverage 0.929; the evaluator's qualification outcome is ``qualified''.\n578\tThe gain is regime-dependent: the reporter only ties the naive interval under halving on real outcomes and at 600 questions, and ties resampling under exhaustive search; in those two real-outcome cases its coverage is 0.89 (halving, within seed noise of nominal) and 0.83 (600 questions, clearly below nominal).\n579\tThe evidence covers one small model, one benchmark and a hand-set width calibration, and does not test effects on ranking decisions.\n580\t\\end{abstract}\n581\t\n582\t\n583\t\n584\t\n585\t\n586\t\n587\t\n588\t\n589\t\n590\t\n591\t\n592\t\n593\t\\section{Introduction}\n594\tPrompt and pipeline optimizers such as DSPy and MIPRO search over instructions, demonstrations and multi-call structures on a benchmark sample \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy,OpsahlOng2024MIPRO}, LLM-driven optimizers propose and rank candidate prompts by their score on a task sample \\citep{Zhou2022APE,Yang2023OPRO}, and in practice the best configuration is often reported with the score it attained on the very sample used to select it. Configuration search is also cast as best-arm identification under a fixed evaluation budget \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}. The reported number is the maximum, over many candidates, of noisy estimates, and the candidate that attains it was chosen \\emph{because} its estimate was high. It is therefore a biased estimate of that candidate's accuracy on new questions, and the usual interval around it ignores the selection. This is the selection bias long documented for model selection, where the score used to pick among many candidates is an optimistic performance estimate \\citep{Cawley2010Overfitting}. Whether comparisons and decisions built on such numbers are trustworthy depends on how large the gap is and on what controls it.\n595\t\n596\tWe ask two questions. \\textbf{(Q1)} For a system whose configuration is chosen on a finite question set, how far is the reported score from its accuracy on fresh questions, what governs the gap, and does it threaten conclusions drawn from reported scores? \\textbf{(Q2)} Can a better reporting rule be designed, and how should its reliability be tested?\n597\t\n598\tOur answer to Q2 is a post-hoc \\emph{reporter} that never touches the search: it receives only the evaluation log of (question, configuration, outcome) triples, the selected configuration and basic metadata, and returns a point estimate and a 90\\% interval for the selected configuration's fresh-question accuracy. The reporter fits a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model with empirical-Bayes variance components (EB-IRT). Because both searchers we study choose using only observed outcomes, selection is ignorable for the likelihood, so one fitting routine serves exhaustive search and successive halving. Shrinkage of configuration effects addresses the winner's curse directly; the interval is formed from posterior quantiles, which are the optimal report under the interval score \\citep{Gneiting2007Scoring}.\n599\t\n600\tWe test reliability with an evaluator that owns the data-generating worlds, the searchers and the truth, so that coverage and interval score are measured against known fresh-question accuracy. Worlds are synthetic latent-difficulty worlds and cached real outcomes of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct \\citep{Qwen2024Qwen25} on MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUPro} under 24 prompt configurations, plus three negative controls.\n601\t\n602\t\\textbf{Contributions.} (i) A quantification of the optimism of best-of-search reporting and of the coverage of its Wilson interval across question budget, world and searcher (Section~\\ref{sec:gap}). (ii) A searcher-agnostic EB-IRT reporter with a reimplementable specification (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (iii) A controlled evaluation protocol with known truth, null and constant-world controls, and seed-level uncertainty, applied to four reporters in a sealed final run (Sections~\\ref{sec:protocol}--\\ref{sec:controls}). (iv) A candid map of where the reporter fails to improve on simpler reporters: real-outcome halving, large question budgets, and exhaustive search where question resampling is as good.\n603\t\n604\t\\textbf{Scope.} The worlds, searchers, baseline reporters and scoring were supplied as a fixed evaluation harness, and the real-outcome tensors were produced beforehand by the task authors; this study ran neither LLM inference nor a DSPy/MIPRO search. Claims are conditional on one 0.5B model, one benchmark, one 24-configuration prompt family and two searchers. We do not test whether the optimism changes the \\emph{ranking} of systems (Section~\\ref{sec:limits}).\n605\t\n606\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n607\t\\centering\n608\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/overview_schematic.png}\n609\t\\caption{Reporting after search. A benchmark sample of $N$ questions is used to search $K$ configurations; the search log and the selected configuration are passed to a reporter, which returns a point estimate and a 90\\% interval for the selected configuration's accuracy on fresh questions. We compare the naive Wilson interval, a Bonferroni-widened interval, a question-resampling optimism correction, and the EB-IRT posterior interval, all scored against known truth.}\n610\t\\label{fig:overview}\n611\t\\end{figure}\n612\t\n613\t\\section{Related Work}\n614\t\\textbf{Adaptive data analysis.} Reusing a holdout set across adaptively chosen analyses invalidates standard inference. \\citet{Dwork2014PreservingSV,Dwork2015GeneralizationIA} showed that differential-privacy-style noise preserves validity, \\citet{Dwork2015TheRH} gave the reusable holdout, \\citet{Feldman2017CalibratingNT} calibrated the noise to variance, and \\citet{Hardt2014Hard} showed that preventing false discovery in fully interactive analysis is computationally hard. The Ladder \\citep{Blum2015Ladder} reports leaderboard scores of repeated submissions through a thresholded mechanism. These methods change \\emph{what is released} to the analyst. Our reporter instead leaves the search untouched and corrects the report afterwards from the log; it does not provide their worst-case guarantees, and we do not study adaptive reporting across several released scores.\n615\t\n616\t\\textbf{Inference after selection.} Post-selection inference constructs valid intervals for selected targets \\citep{Berk2013ValidPSI}; \\citet{Andrews2024InferenceOnWinners} study inference on the best-performing unit, the direct statistical analogue of a best-of-search score; and \\citet{Chernozhukov2013Gaussian} give Gaussian approximations for maxima that underlie bootstrap critical values. Empirical-Bayes shrinkage \\citep{Efron1973Stein} and its connection to selection bias \\citep{Efron2011Tweedie} motivate our use of a hierarchical model. The bootstrap optimism correction \\citep{Efron1997Bootstrap632} motivates the question-resampling baseline. In machine learning, the optimism of a score used to choose among candidates is a documented source of selection bias in model selection \\citep{Cawley2010Overfitting}, and benchmark overfitting under repeated reuse has been probed empirically, on image classification by collecting a fresh test set \\citep{Recht2019ImageNet} and by studying how the number of classes affects overfitting from test-set reuse \\citep{Feldman2019MultipleClasses}. We study the same optimism for searched LLM configurations, with ground truth supplied by the evaluator.\n617\t\n618\t\\textbf{Prompt search and LLM evaluation.} DSPy \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy} and MIPRO \\citep{OpsahlOng2024MIPRO} automate prompt and pipeline optimization, LLM-driven optimizers generate and select instructions by their score on task examples \\citep{Zhou2022APE,Yang2023OPRO}, best-arm formulations bound evaluation cost \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}, successive halving \\citep{Jamieson2016SuccessiveHalving} is a standard budget allocator, performance is sensitive to prompt formatting \\citep{Sclar2023Quantifying}, multi-prompt evaluation can be made sample-efficient \\citep{Polo2024EfficientMultiPrompt}, item response theory has been used to analyse machine-learning classifiers at the instance level \\citep{MartinezPlumed2019IRTinAI}, and error bars on benchmark scores have been advocated \\citep{Miller2024ErrorBars}. None of these works supplies a post-search score with selection-aware uncertainty. We use the Wilson interval \\citep{Wilson1927Probable} for the baselines.\n619\t\n620\t\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n621\t\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\n622\tA system is searched over $K$ configurations on $N$ benchmark questions. A searcher produces a log $\\mathcal L=\\{(q_i,k_i,y_i)\\}$ of question indices, configuration indices and binary outcomes, and selects $\\hat k$ as a function of $\\mathcal L$ only. The target is the selected configuration's accuracy $\\theta_{\\hat k}$ on fresh questions, which the evaluator knows. A reporter receives $\\mathcal L$, $\\hat k$, $N$, $K$, the searcher tag and an anonymous subject code per question, and returns $(\\hat\\theta,\\ell,u)$ with $0\\le \\ell\\le\\hat\\theta\\le u\\le 1$. We score the interval with the interval score at level $1-\\alpha=0.90$,\n623\t\\begin{equation}\n624\t\\IS_\\alpha(\\ell,u;\\theta)=(u-\\ell)+\\tfrac{2}{\\alpha}\\,(\\ell-\\theta)_+ + \\tfrac{2}{\\alpha}\\,(\\theta-u)_+ ,\n625\t\\end{equation}\n626\twhich is a proper scoring rule minimized in expectation by the $\\alpha/2$ and $1-\\alpha/2$ predictive quantiles \\citep{Gneiting2007Scoring}. A missed replication costs $20$ per unit of miss distance, so over-narrow intervals are penalized heavily. We also report coverage, mean width, signed bias of $\\hat\\theta$ and RMSE. A reporter that fails on a replication receives the worst-case score $20$.\n627\t\n628\t\\subsection{Design rationale}\n629\tThe naive reporter returns the selected configuration's observed accuracy $\\hat p_{\\hat k}$ with a Wilson interval. Two effects make it unreliable. First, $\\hat p_{\\hat k}$ is the maximum of noisy estimates, so $\\mathbb E[\\hat p_{\\hat k}-\\theta_{\\hat k}]>0$ (winner's curse); the effect grows when true differences between configurations are small relative to sampling noise, as in a null world. Second, the Wilson interval treats $\\hat p_{\\hat k}$ as a fixed-configuration estimate and ignores selection, so it is too short for its nominal level. We expect an estimator that pools information across configurations and questions to shrink the winner's estimate toward the bulk, and a posterior over $\\theta_{\\hat k}$ to give an interval whose length reflects how little the log says about the selected configuration.\n630\t\n631\t\\subsection{EB-IRT reporter}\n632\t\n633\t\n634\t\\textbf{Model.} With $\\sigma$ the logistic function, each logged outcome follows\n635\t\\begin{equation}\n636\ty_{qk}\\sim\\mathrm{Bernoulli}\\big(\\sigma(a_k-b_q)\\big),\\quad a_k\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\tau^2),\\quad b_q\\sim\\mathcal N(m_{s(q)},s^2),\n637\t\\end{equation}\n638\tThis is a Rasch-type item response model with hierarchical priors, whose item parameters are classically estimated by marginal maximum likelihood with EM \\citep{Bock1981MML}; our fit below instead alternates Newton sweeps over configuration and question parameters with Laplace-corrected variance updates. Here $a_k$ is a configuration effect, $b_q$ a question difficulty and $m_{s}$ the mean difficulty of subject $s$ (the question's subject code; synthetic worlds have a single code). Subject means are shrunk toward the global mean with five pseudo-questions. The fresh-question accuracy of configuration $k$ is $\\theta_k=\\mathbb E_b[\\sigma(a_k-b)]$, where $b$ is drawn from the mixture of subjects weighted by the observed subject frequencies.\n639\t\n640\t\\textbf{Empirical-Bayes fit.} Using only the sparse triples, we alternate damped Newton sweeps for $(a,b)$ with variance updates $\\tau^2\\leftarrow\\frac1K\\big(\\sum_k\\hat a_k^2+\\sum_k S_k^{-1}\\big)$ and $s^2\\leftarrow\\frac1N\\big(\\sum_q(\\hat b_q-m_{s(q)})^2+\\sum_q H_q^{-1}\\big)$, where $S_k$ is the Schur-complement posterior precision of $a_k$ after integrating out difficulties and $H_q$ the diagonal Hessian for $b_q$. These Laplace corrections stop $\\tau^2$ from collapsing or inflating. Fitting uses bincount-based vector operations, so exhaustive and halving logs share one code path; the fit stops after at most 30 sweeps or a 1.5\\,s wall-clock budget.\n641\t\n642\t\\textbf{Ignorability.} Exhaustive search and successive halving choose which entries to observe and which configuration to select using only previously observed outcomes. The likelihood of the log is therefore unaffected by the design, and a likelihood-based posterior is valid under either searcher without a searcher-specific correction, provided the model is adequately specified. This is a motivation, not a proof for the real-outcome worlds, whose subject-by-configuration structure a one-dimensional difficulty model only approximates.\n643\t\n644\t\\textbf{Posterior interval.} We draw 400 samples of $(a_{\\hat k},\\text{level shift},\\log s)$ from Laplace approximations, integrate over fresh difficulties with 21 Gauss--Hermite nodes and each subject, and obtain draws of $\\theta_{\\hat k}$. The estimate is their mean $\\hat\\theta$; with $q_{0.05},q_{0.95}$ the posterior quantiles, the reported interval is\n645\t\\begin{equation}\n646\t\\ell=\\hat\\theta-\\kappa\\,(\\hat\\theta-q_{0.05})_+,\\qquad u=\\hat\\theta+\\kappa\\,(q_{0.95}-\\hat\\theta)_+ ,\n647\t\\end{equation}\n648\twith a width factor $\\kappa(N,K)=\\kappa_0\\,\\big(1+0.25\\min(1,\\sqrt{50/N})\\big)\\big(1+0.03\\log_2(K/2)\\big)$, where $\\kappa_0=1.20$ when the log carries subject codes (real outcomes) and $1.12$ otherwise. The constants are fixed in the source; they are design priors that widen the interval to compensate for ignored hyperparameter uncertainty and misspecification, not values fitted to the final worlds. Over the cases studied $\\kappa$ ranges from 1.43 to 1.63 (Table~\\ref{tab:kappa} in the Supplement), so intervals are roughly 40--60\\% wider than raw posterior quantiles. Because $\\kappa$ acts only around $\\hat\\theta$, bias comparisons with the naive estimate reflect the posterior mean alone, whereas interval width and coverage reflect the posterior and $\\kappa$ jointly; we did not evaluate $\\kappa=1$.\n649\t\n650\t\\textbf{Degenerate worlds.} If every outcome is zero the reporter returns estimate $0$ and upper bound $\\min(3/n_{\\hat k},0.02)$; if $\\hat k$ was never observed it returns the pooled mean $\\pm0.25$; any exception falls back to a widened Wilson interval.\n651\t\n652\t\\subsection{Compared reporters}\\label{sec:baselines}\n653\tAll baselines are computed by the evaluator from the same log. \\emph{Naive Wilson}: observed accuracy of $\\hat k$ with a 90\\% Wilson interval. \\emph{Bonferroni-Wilson}: the same estimate with the Wilson critical value taken at level $1-\\alpha/(2K)$. \\emph{Resampling}: 100 random half-splits; on each, the best configuration on one half is re-scored on the other, the mean score gap $g$ is rescaled to $\\hat p_{\\hat k}-g/\\sqrt2$, and a Wilson interval is placed around it.\n654\t\n655\t\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n656\t\n657\t\n658\t\n659\t\n660\t\\textbf{Worlds.} \\emph{Synthetic} worlds draw a base accuracy uniformly from $[0.25,0.75]$ and per-configuration accuracies with spread $0.03$; outcomes follow a Gaussian copula with latent correlation $0.5$ across configurations, and the truth is the selected configuration's accuracy. \\emph{Real} worlds use a cached $1500\\times24$ binary tensor of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct answers on MMLU-Pro (24 prompt configurations: four instruction wordings $\\times$ three demonstration settings $\\times$ two answer-prefix formats; revisions in the Supplement). Each replication samples $N$ questions without replacement, permutes configuration labels, searches, and takes as truth the selected configuration's accuracy on the remaining pool questions. \\emph{Controls}: N1 is a synthetic world with identical configurations; N2 is the real pool with each question's configuration columns reassigned by a random derangement to break configuration effects; Z1 is an all-zero world with $K=8$. In any null world no configuration is better, so a reporter should neither invent signal nor lose coverage.\n661\t\n662\t\\textbf{Cases and searchers.} Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. \\emph{Exhaustive} search evaluates every configuration on every question and selects the highest total; \\emph{successive halving} works with a nominal budget of $N\\!\\cdot\\!K$ evaluations over $\\lceil\\log_2K\\rceil$ rounds, discarding the lower half of the survivors each round, so configurations eliminated early are observed on few questions and the log is sparse. Each case has $R=30$ replications ($R=10$ for Z1).\n663\t\n664\t\\textbf{Aggregation and uncertainty.} Per-case values average replications; the primary aggregate averages P1--P2 (synthetic, weight 0.4) and P3--P6 (real, weight 0.6) within family. The sealed evaluation uses eight seeds (9101--9108), each running all nine cases. We report mean $\\pm$ standard deviation of seed-level values and, for reporter differences, paired seed-level differences. The evaluator records interval score, bias, coverage and width for the EB-IRT reporter, but only the interval scores of the three baselines and the bias of the naive reporter. All other baseline quantities (baseline coverage and width, and the bias of the Bonferroni and resampling reporters) are replay-derived: we replayed the evaluator's own deterministic world, search and baseline code, which reproduced the recorded baseline interval scores and the recorded naive bias exactly (maximum absolute difference 0). The replay-derived values are not checked against any evaluator record. Per-case records carry an unverified diagnostics label from the evaluator, so we treat aggregate metrics as authoritative and per-case values as supporting. The reporter was frozen before the sealed evaluation, and the evaluation completed all replications (completed fraction 1.0).\n665\t\n666\t\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n667\t\\subsection{Optimism of best-of-search reporting}\\label{sec:gap}\n668\t\n669\t\n670\t\n671\t\n672\t\n673\tAveraged over P1--P6 the naive estimate exceeds the fresh-question accuracy by $0.0366\\pm0.0029$ (Table~\\ref{tab:aggregate}). Its nominal 90\\% Wilson interval contains the truth in $0.713\\pm0.048$ of replications, and in no primary case does it reach 0.82 (Table~\\ref{tab:cases}). The Bonferroni-widened interval fixes coverage ($0.962$) but spends width: $0.190$ against $0.129$ for EB-IRT.\n674\t\n675\t\\emph{Question budget.} On the three real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) the naive bias falls monotonically with $N$: $0.051$ at $N=60$ (P5), $0.022$ at $N=200$ (P3), $0.009$ at $N=600$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a). The optimism at $N=60$ is of the same order as the spread of the 24 configurations' pool accuracies ($0.107$ to $0.166$ in the final pool); we did not measure how the optimism relates to true between-configuration differences.\n676\t\n677\t\\emph{World.} Optimism is larger when configurations do not truly differ: in the synthetic null N1 the naive bias is $0.067$ against $0.049$ in P1 (same $N$, $K$, searcher), and in the permuted real null N2 it is $0.034$ against $0.022$ in P3. This is the winner's-curse pattern expected when selection is among near-equals; we did not test it causally.\n678\t\n679\t\\emph{Searcher and $K$.} Under halving the naive bias is $0.054$ (P2) and $0.025$ (P4), versus $0.049$ (P1) and $0.022$ (P3) under exhaustive search in matched worlds. The matched paired differences ($+0.004\\pm0.012$ and $+0.003\\pm0.008$ across seeds) are small relative to seed spread; aggregated over searcher-specific cases the naive bias is $0.036$ under both. We cannot separate the effect of $K$ because it is confounded with the world (32 synthetic, 24 real, 8 constant).\n680\t\n681\t\\subsection{Main comparison: sealed final evaluation}\n682\t\n683\t\n684\t\n685\t\n686\t\\begin{table}[t]\n687\t\\centering\n688\t\\caption{Sealed final evaluation, primary cases P1--P6 (0.4 synthetic / 0.6 real), mean $\\pm$ standard deviation over eight seeds. The interval score is at level 0.90 (lower is better); coverage should be near 0.90. Bonferroni-Wilson shares the naive point estimate. Baseline coverage, width and the resampling bias are replay-derived from the evaluator's code, not evaluator-recorded (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).}\n689\t\\label{tab:aggregate}\n690\t\\small\n691\t\n692\t\n693\t\n694\t\n695\t\n696\t\n697\t\n698\t\n699\t\n700\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n701\t\\toprule\n702\tReporter & Interval score $\\downarrow$ & Coverage & Mean width & Signed bias \\\\\n703\t\\midrule\n704\tNaive Wilson & 0.2202$\\pm$0.0234 & 0.713$\\pm$0.048 & 0.109 & 0.0366$\\pm$0.0029 \\\\\n705\tBonferroni-Wilson & 0.1974$\\pm$0.0036 & 0.962$\\pm$0.012 & 0.190 & 0.0366$\\pm$0.0029 \\\\\n706\tResampling & 0.2461$\\pm$0.0251 & 0.748$\\pm$0.020 & 0.104 & $-$0.0209$\\pm$0.0030 \\\\\n707\t\\textbf{EB-IRT (ours)} & 0.1449$\\pm$0.0040 & 0.929$\\pm$0.008 & 0.129 & 0.0121$\\pm$0.0027 \\\\\n708\t\\bottomrule\n709\t\\end{tabular}\n710\t\\end{table}\n711\t\n712\tEB-IRT attains a mean interval score of $0.1449$ against $0.2202$ (naive), $0.1974$ (Bonferroni-Wilson) and $0.2461$ (resampling) (Table~\\ref{tab:aggregate}), with coverage $0.929$ and RMSE $0.0348$. Its score is lower than each baseline's in all eight seeds; paired seed-level differences are $0.075\\pm0.021$ (naive), $0.053\\pm0.004$ (Bonferroni) and $0.101\\pm0.023$ (resampling). Its aggregate bias, $0.0121\\pm0.0027$, removes about two thirds of the naive optimism. The evaluator's qualification outcome for this run is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. Gate diagnostics beyond that outcome are not available to us, and the outcome does not by itself establish validity beyond the studied worlds.\n713\t\n714\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n715\t\\centering\n716\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/per_case_interval_score.pdf}\n717\t\\caption{Mean interval score per case (lower is better) for four reporters; bars show mean over eight seeds, whiskers one standard deviation across seeds. (a) Synthetic worlds including the null N1. (b) Real outcomes including the permuted null N2. The resampling bar for P2 is clipped (value 0.59).}\n718\t\\label{fig:perCase}\n719\t\\end{figure}\n720\t\n721\t\\begin{table}[t]\n722\t\\centering\n723\t\\caption{Per-case interval score (mean over eight seeds; best per row in bold) and coverage of the naive and EB-IRT intervals. ``exh.'' exhaustive search, ``halv.'' successive halving. Seed standard deviations are in the Supplement.}\n724\t\\label{tab:cases}\n725\t\\small\n726\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n727\t\n728\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4324b24f-928d-465d-b634-99a0059a2722","uuid":"9d8a3c19-e848-4f4c-90ef-09619866a646","timestamp":"2026-11-11T15:02:12.515Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper_evidence_audit_packet.md","content":"# Agentic evidence audit packet\n\n- generated_at: `2026-11-11T15:02:07.242109+00:00`\n- paper_dir: `work/results/a06/claude/20261111_111240/.paper_evidence_audit_workspaces-1000/agentic-evidence-audit-1000-20261111_142659-paper_evidence_audit_round_3-3h2fpxzn/workspace`\n\nUse this packet as the default entrypoint. Read raw artifacts only when this packet is insufficient.\nThe goal is narrow: check whether the paper's visible claims remain consistent with claims_grounding.json, paper_data.json, and the scientific-evidence trail.\n\n## Expected audit focus\n\n- visible claims in `paper.tex` that overstate, contradict, or drift from `claims_grounding.json`\n- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n- mismatches between visible manuscript text and the resolved/anchored evidence trail\n- cases where the evidence trail exists but does not justify the paper's wording or numeric precision\n- contradictions between `paper.tex`, `claims